**Overall strategy (brief reflection)**  

The pendulum must be driven toward the upright configuration (θ ≈ 0).  
From the observation we can recover the angle θ = atan2(y, x) and its time‑derivative θ̇ (the supplied angular‑velocity component).  
A simple, model‑free controller can be built by applying a torque that “pushes” the pendulum in the direction that reduces the angle error while also damping the motion.  
Thus:

* When the pendulum is on the left side of the vertical (θ > 0, i.e. x < 0, y > 0) we should apply a **negative** torque to rotate it clockwise toward θ = 0.  
* When it is on the right side (θ < 0, i.e. x > 0, y > 0) we apply a **positive** torque to rotate it counter‑clockwise.  
* When the pendulum is below the pivot (y < 0) we need a larger torque to “swing‑up” because gravity is working against us; the sign is still determined by the side (left/right).  
* If the angular velocity already points in the correct direction, we can reduce the magnitude of the torque (damping).  
* If the angular velocity points opposite to the desired direction, we apply the maximum torque in the needed direction to overcome the momentum.

The following IF‑THEN‑ELSE rules implement exactly that logic.  
All decisions use only the current observation \((x, y, \dotθ)\) and produce a continuous torque clipped to the admissible range \([-2.0, 2.0]\).

