Even with the new schedule the controller would still apply the **maximum positive torque** for the whole “falling‑down” phase because the angle is > 2 rad and the feed‑forward term adds a small positive bias.  The improvement comes **later**, when |θ| drops below ≈ 1 rad: the gains become milder and the damping term grows, so the controller will start **reducing** the torque instead of holding it at the limit, allowing the pole to coast upward and then be gently stopped near the upright.  This reduces the cumulative torque‑penalty dramatically while still delivering enough energy to swing up.

