### 2️⃣ Which assumptions were right / wrong?

| Assumption | Reality |
|------------|----------|
| **“If I know θ and ω I can just set a constant torque”** | Wrong – the pendulum needs *different* torques depending on how much kinetic + potential energy it already has. |
| **“A single PD law works everywhere”** | Wrong – a linear PD works only when the pole is already near the upright. Far from the upright the dynamics are highly non‑linear and the PD drives the torque to its limits without ever building enough energy. |
| **“An energy‑shaping term with a huge gain (3.0) will quickly give the right speed”** | Partially right – it does push the pole upward, but the gain is far too large for the limited torque range (±2 Nm). The controller saturates, then the sign flips abruptly, producing the “bang‑bang” oscillation we see after the swing‑up. |
| **“Switching at |θ|≈0.3 rad is enough to hand over to a PD”** | Not optimal – the pole still has a lot of residual kinetic energy at that angle, so the PD immediately fights a large ω and ends up saturating again. |

**Take‑away:** we need a *smooth* hand‑over from a **low‑gain energy controller** (that respects the torque limits) to a **well‑tuned linear controller** that only activates when the pole is truly close to the upright **and** its velocity is small.

